---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Related from "../../components/Related.astro";
import TABLES from "../../data/margin-tables.json";
import { liquidationPrice, maintenanceMarginFraction, type MarginTable } from "../../lib/margin.ts";
import { pct, usd } from "../../lib/funding.ts";

/**
 * THE LIQUIDATION-PRICE EXPLAINER.
 *
 * WHY IT IS WORTH A PAGE OF ITS OWN. The formula in general circulation — entry x (1 - 1/L) for
 * a long — omits maintenance margin entirely, which means every calculator repeating it puts
 * the liquidation further from entry than it is. A trader acting on it believes they have room
 * they do not have. This site already quantifies the gap in a table on /tools; what it has
 * never done is explain WHY the two answers differ, which is the part a reader needs in order
 * to trust the smaller number over the one every other calculator shows them.
 *
 * THE WORKED EXAMPLES ARE COMPUTED, NOT TYPED. Every figure below comes out of the same
 * liquidationPrice() the calculators call, over a margin table committed to this repository.
 * A number typed into prose is a number that goes stale silently — this project has shipped
 * that defect before and now has a gate for it — so the only literals here are the inputs.
 *
 * NO STORE READ, ON PURPOSE. The arithmetic does not change when the market moves, so the
 * route takes a committed date. A live price would make the sentence "the same position, at
 * two sizes" harder to follow, not easier.
 */
const tables = TABLES as unknown as Record<string, MarginTable>;
/* The clearest tier boundary in the published set: 10x up to $3M of notional, 5x above it.
   Chosen because the boundary is reachable in an example a reader can hold in their head. */
const table = tables["51"];
const ENTRY = 100;
const REQUESTED_LEVERAGE = 10;

const small = liquidationPrice({ entryPrice: ENTRY, positionSize: 10_000, leverage: REQUESTED_LEVERAGE, side: "long", table });
const large = liquidationPrice({ entryPrice: ENTRY, positionSize: 40_000, leverage: REQUESTED_LEVERAGE, side: "long", table });

const title = "How a liquidation price is actually calculated";
const description =
  "The formula most calculators use leaves out maintenance margin, so it places the " +
  "liquidation further from entry than the venue will. What maintenance margin is, how " +
  "margin tiers change the answer with position size, and both figures worked through.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>How a liquidation price is calculated</h1>
  </div>

  <p class="callout">
    A liquidation price is the price at which a venue closes a position because the collateral
    behind it has fallen to the minimum it will accept. That minimum is called
    <strong> maintenance margin</strong>, and the formula in general circulation leaves it out —
    which puts the liquidation further from entry than the venue actually will. The error is in
    the direction that costs money.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">The formula you will usually be given</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    For a long position, almost every calculator and explainer computes:
  </p>
  <p class="formula"><code>liquidation = entry × (1 − 1 ÷ leverage)</code></p>
  <p class="prose">
    The reasoning is intuitive and wrong. At {REQUESTED_LEVERAGE}× leverage the margin posted is
    a {REQUESTED_LEVERAGE}th of the notional, so a move of a {REQUESTED_LEVERAGE}th against the
    position wipes it out — {usd(ENTRY, 2)} becomes {usd(small.naivePrice, 2)}. That would be
    correct if the venue let the collateral reach exactly zero. No venue does, because a
    position with zero collateral cannot be closed without someone else absorbing the shortfall.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">What maintenance margin adds</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    The venue closes the position while there is still something left: a fixed fraction of the
    notional that must remain at all times. On Hyperliquid that fraction is half the initial
    margin required at the tier's maximum leverage — so a tier permitting
    {" "}{table.marginTiers[0].maxLeverage}× carries a maintenance margin of
    {" "}{pct(maintenanceMarginFraction(table.marginTiers[0].maxLeverage))} of notional. Because
    the position must be closed while that fraction survives, the liquidation price moves
    <strong> towards</strong> entry:
  </p>
  <p class="formula"><code>liquidation = entry × (1 − 1 ÷ leverage) ÷ (1 − maintenance margin fraction)</code></p>

  <h2 class="section">The same position, worked twice</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    A long on a contract trading at {usd(ENTRY, 0)}, opened at {REQUESTED_LEVERAGE}× on the
    venue's {JSON.stringify(table.description).slice(1, -1)} margin table. The only difference
    between the two columns is size.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th class="num">{usd(small.notional)} position</th><th class="num">{usd(large.notional)} position</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Entry price</td><td class="num">{usd(ENTRY, 0)}</td><td class="num">{usd(ENTRY, 0)}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Leverage requested</td><td class="num">{REQUESTED_LEVERAGE}×</td><td class="num">{REQUESTED_LEVERAGE}×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Leverage the tier permits</td><td class="num">{small.effectiveLeverage}×</td><td class="num">{large.effectiveLeverage}×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maintenance margin fraction</td><td class="num">{pct(small.maintenanceMarginFraction)}</td><td class="num">{pct(large.maintenanceMarginFraction)}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Common formula says</td><td class="num">{usd(small.naivePrice, 2)}</td><td class="num">{usd(large.naivePrice, 2)}</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Venue liquidates at</strong></td><td class="num"><strong set:text={usd(small.liquidationPrice, 2)} /></td><td class="num"><strong set:text={usd(large.liquidationPrice, 2)} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gap between the two</td><td class="num">{usd(small.differenceAbs, 2)} ({pct(small.differencePct)})</td><td class="num">{usd(large.differenceAbs, 2)} ({pct(large.differencePct)})</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Room from entry, in truth</td><td class="num">{pct(small.distancePct)}</td><td class="num">{pct(large.distancePct)}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="section-note" style="margin-top:10px">
    Every figure in this table is computed on render by the same function the
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/tools/position-size">calculators</a> call, over a margin table
    committed to this project. Nothing here is a number somebody typed in once.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Why the larger position is worse off</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Maximum leverage is not a property of the venue or of the coin. It is a property of the
    <strong> tier</strong>, and tiers step down as the position grows, because a larger position
    is harder to close without moving the price. On the table used above, positions up to
    {" "}{usd(table.marginTiers[1].lowerBound)} may use {table.marginTiers[0].maxLeverage}×;
    above that the cap falls to {table.marginTiers[1].maxLeverage}×, and the maintenance margin
    fraction doubles with it.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Two consequences follow, and neither is visible in the common formula. A request for
    {" "}{REQUESTED_LEVERAGE}× on a position above the boundary is silently clamped to
    {" "}{large.effectiveLeverage}×, so the position is smaller than intended relative to
    margin. And the liquidation price sits {pct(large.distancePct)} from entry rather than
    {" "}{pct(small.distancePct)} — the same order, at a different size, with different room.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Tier starts at</th><th class="num">Maximum leverage</th><th class="num">Maintenance margin</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {table.marginTiers.map((t) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{usd(t.lowerBound)}</td>
            <td class="num">{t.maxLeverage}×</td>
            <td class="num">{pct(maintenanceMarginFraction(t.maxLeverage))}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section">Cross and isolated margin</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Everything above assumes the position stands on its own collateral — <em>isolated</em>
    margin. Under <em>cross</em> margin the account's whole free balance backs every position,
    so the liquidation price of any one of them depends on what the others are doing, and it
    moves when they do. The arithmetic here is the isolated case because it is the one that can
    be stated for a single position; under cross margin the same formula gives the price at
    which that position alone would exhaust its share, which is a floor rather than an answer.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">What this does not include</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Funding payments are deducted from collateral while a position is open, which moves the
    liquidation price gradually in the direction of the payment — a long paying funding is
    liquidated slightly earlier each hour it holds. Fees do the same. Neither is in the formula
    above, and over a long hold at a high rate the effect is not negligible:
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/learn/funding-rate">what funding costs</a> is the other half of
    the same arithmetic.
  </p>

  <Related
    title="Where to go next"
    items={[
      { href: "/tools/position-size", label: "Position size calculator", note: "Your entry, size and leverage, with the tier-aware liquidation price and the gap against the common formula." },
      { href: "/tools/leverage", label: "Leverage calculator", note: "What a given leverage does to margin, room and the maintenance requirement." },
      { href: "/tools", label: "All calculators", note: "Including the table of how far the common formula sits from the published one, per contract." },
      { href: "/learn/liquidation-heatmap", label: "Liquidation heatmaps", note: "What happens when this arithmetic is applied to a whole book instead of one position." },
      { href: "/methodology", label: "Methodology", note: "Where the margin tables come from and how they are kept current." },
      { href: "/learn", label: "All explainers", note: "Funding, liquidation maps, liquidation price and open interest." },
    ]}
  />

  <style>
    h2.section { margin-top: 32px; }
    /* THE TWO FORMULAS ARE THE CONTENT OF THIS PAGE, and as inline <code> inside a paragraph
       they wrapped mid-expression on a phone: measured at 375px, the first one broke after
       "1 ÷" and rendered as two separate tinted boxes with a gap between them, which reads as
       two fragments rather than one expression. A block of its own cannot wrap into pieces. */
    .formula {
      max-width: 72ch; margin: 0 0 var(--gap-block); padding: 12px var(--inset-panel);
      background: var(--surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
      border-radius: var(--r-md); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text);
    }
    .formula code { background: none; padding: 0; white-space: nowrap; }
  </style>
</Base>
